WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.489
User-generated content includes comments, profiles, descriptions, support
tickets, forum posts, markdown, rich text, uploaded files, and CMS-style
fields.

2
00:00:09.489 --> 00:00:11.913
Some of this content should be plain text.

3
00:00:11.913 --> 00:00:16.485
Some applications need limited formatting, links, lists, or embedded
media.

4
00:00:16.485 --> 00:00:25.628
The first security decision is the content policy: what formatting is
actually allowed, who can create it, who can view it, and which contexts
will render it.

5
00:00:25.628 --> 00:00:32.554
Sanitization at a high level means preserving useful allowed content while
removing or neutralizing unsafe content.

6
00:00:32.554 --> 00:00:37.957
It should rely on vetted libraries and clear policies rather than ad hoc
string replacement.

7
00:00:37.957 --> 00:00:45.853
Browser parsing is complex, and simple replacements tend to miss contexts,
encodings, malformed input, or future rendering changes.

8
00:00:45.853 --> 00:00:53.611
A durable approach defines allowed elements, allowed attributes, URL
rules, and output handling rules in one maintainable policy.

9
00:00:53.611 --> 00:00:57.628
Storing raw versus sanitized content is an architectural decision.

10
00:00:57.628 --> 00:01:06.148
Storing raw content can allow reprocessing if the policy changes, but it
requires strict controls around where raw content is viewed or exported.

11
00:01:06.148 --> 00:01:12.866
Storing sanitized content can simplify rendering, but policy changes may
require re-sanitization or migration.

12
00:01:12.866 --> 00:01:16.191
Some applications store both with careful access rules.

13
00:01:16.191 --> 00:01:21.247
The important point is to make the choice deliberately and document the
consequences.

14
00:01:21.247 --> 00:01:27.412
Rich content should be reviewed whenever policy, libraries, templates, or
frontend rendering changes.

15
00:01:27.412 --> 00:01:32.122
A sanitized field may be safe for one page and unsafe if reused in another
context.

16
00:01:32.122 --> 00:01:38.009
Markdown conversion, preview features, imports, and admin editing can all
create alternate paths.

17
00:01:38.009 --> 00:01:44.520
The safest teams treat rich content as a long-lived trust boundary, not as
a one-time cleanup step at input time.
